INT. BONDED WAREHOUSE, WINE CELLAR LEVEL - NIGHT

Brick vaults, racks of dusty bottles in wooden crates. A single bare bulb. Stairs lead up into darkness.

LEONIE BARTHOLOMEW-ACHEBE (30s, fraud investigator, leather satchel) kneels at a crate with a flashlight. NATHANIEL "NATE" OBUYA (40s, warehouse manager, lanyard) hovers behind her. DOROTHEA PRUITT-LANE (60s, private owner, silver cane) stands at the foot of the stairs. And leaning in the arch of the next vault, a bottle in his hand, is MAXIMILIAN VOSS-KAGAN (50s, collector, silk scarf).

LEONIE
These are labeled 1982.

NATE
They are 1982.

LEONIE
The cork says 2014.

NATE
Corks are replaced.

LEONIE
Not with a laser-etched date.

DOROTHEA
Nathaniel, what is she talking about?

NATE
Mrs. Pruitt-Lane, it's a routine--

LEONIE
(over him)
It's not routine. Someone's been decanting new wine into old bottles.

MAXIMILIAN
How very cinematic.

DOROTHEA
Max, be quiet.

MAXIMILIAN
Dorothea, I'm only observing.

NATE
(to Maximilian)
You told me they were authentic.

MAXIMILIAN
They are authentic bottles.

LEONIE
(to Maximilian)
You sold three hundred of them.

MAXIMILIAN
I sold three hundred bottles of excellent wine.

DOROTHEA
Of 1982.

MAXIMILIAN
Of excellent wine, Dorothea.

NATE
She's going to the police.

LEONIE
Nate, you're making it worse.

NATE
(to Leonie)
Aren't you?

LEONIE
(to Dorothea)
Mrs. Pruitt-Lane, your name is on every certificate.

DOROTHEA
My signature, yes.

LEONIE
Your signature authenticates every fake.

DOROTHEA
I authenticated what I was shown.

MAXIMILIAN
She authenticated what she was told.

NATE
By who?

MAXIMILIAN
By you, Nathaniel, you wrote the provenance.

NATE
You told me what to write!

DOROTHEA
(sharply)
Both of you.

A long silence. The bulb buzzes. Somewhere above, a floorboard creaks. Leonie lifts her flashlight toward the stairs.

LEONIE
Who else is here?

NATE
No one.

MAXIMILIAN
Just us.

DOROTHEA
Don't lie, Max.

The beam finds nothing, then a shadow moving on the wall.

LEONIE
(low)
Nobody move.

MAXIMILIAN
That's the cat.

NATE
We don't have a cat.

DOROTHEA
We have a cat.

NATE
Since when?

DOROTHEA
Since Tuesday. Nathaniel, you should read the memos.

A clank from above. All four turn toward the stairs.

LEONIE
(whispering)
Give me your phones. All of you.

MAXIMILIAN
Absolutely not.

DOROTHEA
Give her yours, Max.

NATE
Mine's dead.

LEONIE
Then you're no use.

NATE
That's harsh.

MAXIMILIAN
That's accurate.

DOROTHEA
Max, the phone.

Maximilian sighs. Slides the phone across the stone. Leonie pockets it. Her flashlight flickers and dies. In the darkness, footsteps descend the stairs, heavy, unhurried.

NATE
(whispering)
That's not the cat.
